1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:02,560
Is kanji really necessary?

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:04,000
What if Japanese used only

3
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:05,760
hiragana and katakana?

4
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,190
Look at this sentence.

5
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:09,380
Haha wa hana ga suki.

6
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:12,180
It means, "Mother likes flowers."

7
00:00:12,270 --> 00:00:13,970
Now, let's remove the kanji,

8
00:00:13,990 --> 00:00:16,020
and write everything in hiragana.

9
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:18,330
Haha wa hana ga suki.

10
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:20,000
Wait...

11
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:21,040
why does this suddenly

12
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:22,650
look so confusing?

13
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:24,000
Japanese has many

14
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:25,600
words that sound alike.

15
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:27,520
Without kanji, readers may need

16
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:29,280
more context to figure out which

17
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,120
words and meanings are intended.

18
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:33,040
Kanji gives your eyes an important

19
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:34,720
clue, helping you recognize

20
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:36,650
meaningful words more easily.

21
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:38,090
Here's another example.

22
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:41,230
Niwa ni wa niwa niwatori ga iru.

23
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,250
Now look at it with kanji.

24
00:00:43,370 --> 00:00:46,430
Niwa ni wa niwa niwatori ga iru.

25
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:47,840
"There are two chickens

26
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:48,890
in the garden."

27
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:50,080
See how the kanji helps

28
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,600
make the meaning clearer?

29
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:54,170
So, is kanji really necessary?

30
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:55,960
Japanese can be written without

31
00:00:55,980 --> 00:00:57,680
kanji in some situations.

32
00:00:57,700 --> 00:00:59,360
But kanji helps make many

33
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:00,920
everyday sentences easier

34
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:02,600
to distinguish and read.

35
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:05,280
Hiragana, katakana, and kanji each

36
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:07,200
have their own important role.

37
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,120
So, don't look at kanji as just

38
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:10,960
another obstacle to overcome.

39
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:12,200
Every character you learn

40
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:13,720
helps you understand a little

41
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,520
more of the Japanese language.

42
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:17,000
Don't fear kanji.

43
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:17,760
Learn to see the

44
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:18,920
meaning behind it.

45
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:20,280
Follow me, and let's

46
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,010
master Japanese together!

47
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:23,110
Mata ne!
